🏰 The Big Analogy: Think of a CAR

Before code, let's set up the mental picture. A car has:

Stuff hidden under the hood (engine wiring) 🔒 → Encapsulation
You just press the accelerator, don't need to know HOW the engine works 🎭 → Abstraction
A "Sports Car" is a TYPE of "Car" — inherits basic car features 🧬 → Inheritance
Pressing "brake" does different things in a bicycle vs a car vs a plane 🎪 → Polymorphism

Now let's go deep on each, one by one 👇

1️⃣ Encapsulation — "Keep Your Stuff Private, Bro" 🔐

The idea: Bundle data (variables) + methods that operate on that data into ONE unit (a class), and HIDE the internal details from the outside world. You control access using private, public, protected.

Real analogy: Think of your bank account 🏦. You can't directly go into the bank's database and change your balance to ₹10 crore. You can only interact through specific "methods" the bank allows — deposit, withdraw. The actual balance variable? Hidden from you.

java
class BankAccount {
    private double balance; // 🔒 hidden, no one can touch this directly

    public BankAccount(double initialBalance) {
        balance = initialBalance;
    }

    // ✅ controlled access — the ONLY way to change balance
    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance) {
            balance -= amount;
        } else {
            System.out.println("❌ Insufficient funds!");
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance; // controlled READ access
    }
}

Notice: you CANNOT do account.balance = 999999999; from outside — it's private. You're FORCED to go through deposit()/withdraw(), which have validation logic. That's encapsulation protecting your data from being messed up carelessly. 🛡️

🎤 One-liner: "Encapsulation is bundling data and methods together while restricting direct access to internal state, exposing controlled access through public methods."